---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Install" prose>
  <h1>Install</h1>
  <p>
    Hexpunk ships as <code>@hexpunk/core</code> on npm. Install alongside <code>lit</code>:
  </p>

  <pre><code>bun add @hexpunk/core lit</code></pre>

  <p>
    Then import the elements you want (or the full barrel for everything):
  </p>

  <pre><code>import "@hexpunk/core";</code></pre>

  <p>
    The package side-effects-registers every <code>&lt;hp-*&gt;</code> tag via Lit's
    <code>@customElement</code> decorator on import.
  </p>

  <h2>Tokens</h2>
  <p>
    The dark-theme token set ships as <code>@hexpunk/core/tokens.dark.css</code>. Link or import it
    once at the app root.
  </p>

  <h2>Native HTML baseline</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@hexpunk/core/elements.css</code> grounds raw HTML — prose, links, tables, form
    fields — in the design system, and <code>@hexpunk/core/hex-controls.css</code> carries
    the generated hex form-control patterns. Import both after the tokens:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`import "@hexpunk/core/tokens.dark.css";
import "@hexpunk/core/tokens.light.css";
import "@hexpunk/core/elements.css";
import "@hexpunk/core/hex-controls.css";`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    What elements.css deliberately does <em>not</em> do: restyle <code>&lt;hp-*&gt;</code>
    elements (shadow DOM owns its chrome), repaint native controls as full component
    lookalikes (hex geometry stays with the patterns and components), or force a page-level
    max-width — the shell is yours. Each native element documents its styling on its own
    page inside the component categories.
  </p>
</Layout>
